//
// Phase C4 registry writer: host-only tests for the append-only rule.
//
// The rule: the sweep may APPEND a shape, may add a METHOD to a shape it wrote
// itself, and may do nothing else. The rows already in kernel_registry are what
// the ten shipped LLM deployments resolve against, so a writer that quietly
// re-measured one would change their behaviour without anyone asking.
//
// This is the only thing that checks the rule before the fact. The driver's
// objective check compares the registry against its base commit, which catches
// the breach after the file has already been overwritten and says nothing about
// which guard failed. These provoke each guard directly, against a temporary
// copy of a synthetic registry -- the module's own GEMM_JSON path is repointed
// for the duration, so no test here can reach the real file.
//
// No NPU, no XRT, no Peano: the writer is text manipulation over JSON. It sits
// beside the compile-only kernel gate for the same reason the Phase B seam tests
// do, and it is cheap enough to keep in a PR check.
//
// The test file carries no framework dependency -- pytest is not installed in
// the sandbox venv -- so it runs as a plain script and prints a pass count. The
// count is part of the gate: a file that silently stopped defining a test would
// still print a final line saying everything passed.
//
// Editing this header: lit scans the WHOLE file for its directives, so naming
// one in prose -- even inside a comment -- registers as a second directive and
// leaves the test UNRESOLVED. Describe them, never spell them out.
//
// RUN: python3 %S/test_registry_writer.py | FileCheck %s
//
// CHECK: 8/8 passed
